ype/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465956}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a2bfce5-db39-5ada-92f7-ade37d95ec31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186465956} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0b4c2f3-46b2-5669-ae14-3227a5a0e6f9" :answer-field/key "$Z=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186466821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cbe50951-f70c-5a66-becb-e9afd71bebc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-31968" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $W$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\frac{3}{4}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =W\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $Y$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $Z$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $W$ and $Z$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329683 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-f