{E}_{1}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551262 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f096b5f-39c2-585d-9076-e381a97ad95f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550644} {:answer/id #uuid "95ac74e0-7781-595b-b1cb-5685696d0923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$T$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550745} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9ae38d-468e-5c1b-a879-895b482ed7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550766} {:answer/id #uuid "ace569a1-cdae-5f8a-b5cb-eb5a89edde5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550833} {:answer/id #uuid "c605cbc3-363f-5199-936d-cf502df10aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c605cbc3-363f-5199-936d-cf502df10aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $X$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550902} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f0fef4-e8e5-5e33-982a-88aa1db63724" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3a9d38f9-b939-5805-88eb-ef96ffddb725" :question/math-academy-id "q-42754" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,U=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,X=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $T$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $U$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:=9{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $X$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =X\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $U$ and $X$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551266 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "582190fc-850a-5c44-9e79-a818e78b201e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550584} {:answer/id #uuid "68202c51-a8c7-5b41-8b8e-52288fa600be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550625} {:answer/id #uuid "7873d209-1846-562f-a21c-77b8683916c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550674} {:answer/id #uuid "9266158b-36be-5081-ada4-69f033c39993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550737} {:answer/id #uuid "e7bd5536-867b-57d6-9150-07d885c15b03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550996}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9266158b-36be-5081-ada4-69f033c39993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550737} :answer-field/id #uuid "a94df7ac-0f23-5acc-9a88-bce1ea6077bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3df63019-7de5-5b3e-afc6-d7a9e953df1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130784" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle L=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $L$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[L\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551278 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171d85cf-363f-5e91-8844-7377fbfe4868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550417} {:answer/id #uuid "abc41fe2-4fc6-5168-adaa-26ac614241df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550831} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d97c63-845a-5df4-bd21-ef85ce115fe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550877} {:answer/id #uuid "d05c1129-f39e-5113-b571-d89546b3420c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550927} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b02096-c5f8-533f-a7c6-397872aa44c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "171d85cf-363f-5e91-8844-7377fbfe4868" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550417} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aa55760-fe42-5b20-a3f8-0684f066d9d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5238d337-5418-5670-995a-6cacdf9c34e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-130816" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551286 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32904df2-4f2b-512e-b905-4f4eebcb813e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550488} {:answer/id #uuid "b50b8760-3a6d-55c4-a188-c37cf457dc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550863} {:answer/id #uuid "e8fc2b8c-e26a-5dd7-a42a-b9a4b347b89f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550998} {:answer/id #uuid "f98c45d9-1ef2-58e4-b974-3eb63756e062" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -12 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551036} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcf0b97-a0e4-515d-9606-6b57bb04f711" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551057}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b50b8760-3a6d-55c4-a188-c37cf457dc61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550863} :answer-field/id #uuid "f732bcd0-c8c8-5638-93ed-8a183c2c0d4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6ad7a8de-9677-5fd6-a871-171f20886f2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-130788" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle N=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $N$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[N\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{12} & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:=12{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 12\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{N}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551294 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2009ffbb-834a-5c7a-8a8b-959aa0b24db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550438} {:answer/id #uuid "40c3d139-ddd9-5cf3-80a9-cb1261eea249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550516} {:answer/id #uuid "44c29b3a-55ca-586c-873f-1a2bc7582a6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550528} {:answer/id #uuid "65e51982-290b-5109-8387-cd14e01378eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550619} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a6f7a8-a40b-5e83-8bb3-7321150a3ccc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2009ffbb-834a-5c7a-8a8b-959aa0b24db2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$N$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550438} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e59a97-7099-5d85-88ab-f4f7fe7124bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7abe0f71-31aa-5c86-a7a0-fa2df71ee9ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-42756" :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nH=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,N=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $H$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =H\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $K$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $N$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\nTherefore, only $N$ is not an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551296 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "726b610b-966f-5fad-8937-be3c12d0601d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550654} {:answer/id #uuid "77d890b8-7322-5242-a284-9e74f77fee01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550671} {:answer/id #uuid "d0ae9d64-3c04-543e-9bad-0f91c19f44f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550928} {:answer/id #uuid "db2ac3cb-cacf-5f7f-8f49-0a6d597fce81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550963} {:answer/id #uuid "f6d47fd4-271e-59b7-9dfb-e64e565385a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0ae9d64-3c04-543e-9bad-0f91c19f44f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550928} :answer-field/id #uuid "3af9e33e-f08c-574c-97d5-344ef1178cc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7effc142-a727-5443-a7a6-2e2db67c4b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-32008" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle S=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $S$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[S\\,|\\,I\\,] & =[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}] &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & 1\\end{array}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{S}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551345 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b3907f9-1f0a-5178-9e3a-4ced32f85e38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550545} {:answer/id #uuid "9fb9592e-f39c-550c-bee2-e4351328d285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550778} {:answer/id #uuid "ae49d544-bd4f-5e14-976f-0cf55c49ae03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550838} {:answer/id #uuid "f0dfb83d-8ab3-5bf3-b0c6-cf693a27a6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551017} {:answer/id #uuid "fb20b7d7-1969-5e7b-b0bc-77ea754ddb15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0dfb83d-8ab3-5bf3-b0c6-cf693a27a6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551017} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1767fe4-e53c-5579-92c4-0975b0bb4129" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "dd52658d-d0dc-5c1e-b084-552fde414f6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-130775" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $A$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:=(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $C$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $A$ and $C$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186655467 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00eb3c3c-94da-568e-ae69-ee06c86411b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654885} {:answer/id #uuid "0e5c179e-7f11-59ec-b7e7-7bb56763cb44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654908} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4710de-a115-59e4-9eeb-23a9ed660ac2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$K$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654930} {:answer/id #uuid "7b10d766-7ab7-5b32-90c3-1ccf558fc5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655098} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1a985a-6e11-5316-a9a1-2024af25bdb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ and $K$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00eb3c3c-94da-568e-ae69-ee06c86411b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ and $M$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654885} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a617a39-ee7f-55be-ae5b-f45461251bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "28e143d3-1013-5f6d-8d06-a18ce2052132" :question/math-academy-id "q-130779" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nJ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,K=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,M=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrix $J$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{2}:=7{R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:=7{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The matrix $K$ is not an elementary matrix because it can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $M$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =M\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $J$ and $M$ are elementary matrices."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186655470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b20500d-c8f5-57c4-b71e-b430c298d68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654927} {:answer/id #uuid "51cef294-c60b-517f-a083-12787d972e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655032} {:answer/id #uuid "884d77a2-1be4-5bb6-be7a-de4069d4b01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ and $J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655135} {:answer/id #uuid "9eee48c7-2d4e-573c-97ed-260e82f6b2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$J$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655168} {:answer/id #uuid "d868e1f6-28d0-546f-8f42-cd582d2a64db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$G$ and $H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b20500d-c8f5-57c4-b71e-b430c298d68a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$H$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654927} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9990f2c-7308-505a-8537-819c34c78dc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2c1dbd9e-a730-5529-9179-76eeb9cf2faa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130773" :question/problem "Which of the following are elementary matrices?\n\n$$\nG=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,H=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,J=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "An elementary matrix is a matrix obtained from the identity matrix by applying exactly one elementary row operation. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- The matrices $G$ and $J$ are not elementary matrices because they can't be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying only one elementary row operation.\n\n- The matrix $H$ is an elementary matrix because it can be obtained from ${I}_{2}$ by applying the elementary row operation ${R}_{1}:=(-2){R}_{1},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{2} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{1}:=(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =H\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, only $H$ is an elementary matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186655474 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b543eba-674c-5af8-8bf8-73901976c127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 16 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655070} {:answer/id #uuid "990ffaa0-139d-592e-a121-1e2279fac5c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655158} {:answer/id #uuid "c3dbdbd1-3446-517c-9d69-aa2b487d9eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655236} {:answer/id #uuid "c3fa2b48-6a95-5ad3-8f0c-9d08d9911f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -16 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655237} {:answer/id #uuid "ff92fc5e-2b49-5762-b747-2997c2e73f81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -16\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655346}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3dbdbd1-3446-517c-9d69-aa2b487d9eca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655236} :answer-field/id #uuid "63dba513-1a22-53d5-a807-eb51e70e7837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37f69787-40e9-5f57-8294-76a315d3caa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-130792" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the elementary matrix \n$$\n\\displaystyle K=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 16 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write down $K$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[K\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 16 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+(-16){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{K}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -16 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186655532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8db725-d5de-5a8f-b913-109ebb5b344d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654902} {:answer/id #uuid "0fcd979a-f39a-5942-abcf-95b1408a8ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}73 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654910} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9e5528-be7d-5d69-984d-c585aef558b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655044} {:answer/id #uuid "7bf289f7-3ef3-5735-b86a-ca140a2f0a32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}73 & 9 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655100} {:answer/id #uuid "de13058a-e983-55cb-a110-137d90bad164" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 73\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655284}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fcd979a-f39a-5942-abcf-95b1408a8ae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}73 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654910} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b1a1710-4b39-5d32-a7b9-7c70c2d8ebff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655401}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "be282400-acdd-5a8e-81a1-ee0e202dec24" :question/math-academy-id "q-73652" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{1}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{1}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{1}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & -8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}:={R}_{1}+8{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}^{-1}{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}73 & 8 \\\\ 9 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186655535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064d6be0-20f7-5f2b-ab62-253587d60102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186654894} {:answer/id #uuid "41d51f0c-0a83-5432-a4cf-317a01ce5e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655006} {:answer/id #uuid "69c4ba4e-25a3-50d0-ac95-b149e9c37e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655067} {:answer/id #uuid "8562dbc8-116a-5421-82cc-37cf12ce3af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655124} {:answer/id #uuid "bbeeece0-ac89-5dc4-bfcf-850ca833f730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbeeece0-ac89-5dc4-bfcf-850ca833f730" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655224} :answer-field/id #uuid "375e2018-c208-589f-bba2-4a8ae983477e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186655372}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c641b5c0-be8b-5f57-a96c-eb43733ad5ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-73649" :question/problem "Find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}$, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{E}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that ${E}_{1}$ and ${E}_{2}$ are both elementary matrices.\n\nFirst, we need to find ${E}_{2}^{-1}.$ We write down ${E}_{2}$ and the identity matrix $I$ side-by-side (as shown below) and apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[{E}_{2}\\,|\\,I\\,] & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{array}\\right] &  & {R}_{1}↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0\\end{array}\\right]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{2}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the product ${E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n{E}_{1}{E}_{2}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}]}
